codex
**The comparison is worth sending, but first make its first Sol request a one-worker compatibility check.** If Sol rejects the exact request, that limits the initial exposure to one reservation instead of four.

Use a preselected topic from the registered 26 and retain its answer as the first Sol draw; do not inspect its quality or change the test based on it. If the request succeeds, continue the registered comparison within the same caps. If it fails, stop and report the failure. Run the required $0 preflight first. This preserves the comparison while applying the project’s small-first rule.

Two record corrections are also needed before the send:

- The saved [check output](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-6/model/check-output.txt) says the Sol cap is **$3.50**, while the [design and runner](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-6/model/DESIGN.md) say **$4.50**. Regenerate the check output for the reviewed launcher and confirm the current caps. The stored check digest does match the current code.
- The design’s inherited line saying **effort** is the only request difference should say **model**. The current check compares the new arms that way; the historical request match remains a canonical hash comparison.

The published Sol page supports the stated **$2 input / $10 output per million tokens**, Chat Completions and structured outputs; it does not guarantee this particular request will succeed. [OpenAI model documentation](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-6.1-sol)

The 26 topics remain exposed development material. A positive result would advance a candidate, not pass gate 1. I have not sent a request.

**CONSENSUS: no, because the first Sol request should run alone under the registered cap, and the preflight and reviewed check record must match the launcher before the full send.**
